From b8e5a38061fecabffc8c3fc0a8fe4313e983cf37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:37:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(engine): evaluate an operand TLC evaluates before testing
 elements in it

TLC evaluates both operands of \cap and \, left first, before it reduces
the operation by testing one operand's elements in the other
(OPCODE_cap, OPCODE_setdiff), and evaluates IsFiniteSet's set before it
asks it. Where the operand it tests in is a set it keeps lazy, as
[a : Int, b : {1 \div 0}], the engine evaluated that operand only
through its membership test, so where the other operand was empty it
never did: Cardinality({} \cap [a : Int, b : {1 \div 0}]) found no error
where TLC reports the division by zero, as main did. IsFiniteSet read
Seq(S) from a reference that doesn't evaluate S. Both now run the
operand's parts in TLC's order whether or not there are elements to
test.

The startup fixtures lazy-reduced-*-empty-left-fails and
lazy-finite-seq-part-fails pin each against TLC.
